---
// firma2-project-rail — WHAT THE PROJECT RECORD'S RAIL HOLDS, arranged as three
// tabs: Key facts, Contacts, Comments.
//
// WHY TABS, AND NOT A THIRD AND FOURTH STACKED SECTION. The rail is pinned to
// the fold for the whole page, which is its entire reason for existing: the
// numbers a reader needs in order to READ the main column must not scroll away
// from it. A rail that stacks three sections spends that guarantee — the facts
// hold the fold, and everything under them is below it again, so contacts and
// comments would be a scroll inside a column that exists so you do not have to
// scroll. Tabs keep every one of the three ONE CLICK from the fold instead of
// one, two and three screens down.
//
// THE THREE ARE PEERS, WHICH IS THE TEST TABS HAVE TO PASS. Tabs are the wrong
// control for a primary thing plus two secondary ones — they hide the primary
// half the time. These three are genuinely alternatives: WHAT this project is
// (the scalars), WHO to call about it, and WHAT has been said about it. A
// reader arrives wanting exactly one of them and knows which before the page
// loads. Key facts leads because it is the one the main column is read against.
//
// THE ORDER IS THE FREQUENCY ORDER, not a taxonomy. Facts are consulted on
// every visit; contacts on the visit where something needs doing; comments on
// the visit where a number looks wrong. That is also decreasing tie to the
// page's own question, which is why it survives both readings.
//
// THE COMMENT COUNT RIDES ON THE TAB, and it is the one badge in here. A tab
// whose content might be EMPTY needs to say so from the outside — otherwise the
// only way to learn there is nothing to read is to go and look. Contacts takes
// no badge: it is always three, so a count there is a number that never moves.
// Key facts takes none for the same reason. The badge only renders above zero;
// a "0" would be the same wasted trip with an extra glyph.
//
// EVERY PANEL IS A COMPONENT, and this file is their manifest — it owns the tab
// wiring and nothing else. No panel knows it is in a tab except in the one way
// it has to: each hides its own heading, because the tab label already says it.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: esa-tab-layout IS the lego and this composes it rather than
// re-implementing it — the ARIA tablist, the roving Arrow/Home/End keyboard
// navigation, the active-tab state, the badges and the panel swap are all the
// lego's. It is a Lit web component (`.ts` in the catalog), so it is registered
// in a client script and used as a custom element; the panels are slotted light
// DOM, which is what lets three .astro components live inside a runtime tab
// control with no runtime rendering of their own. The only bespoke line here is
// the :not(:defined) guard — see the note on it below.
import Firma2ProjectFacts from './firma2-project-facts.astro';
import Firma2ProjectContacts from './firma2-project-contacts.astro';
import Firma2ProjectComments from './firma2-project-comments.astro';
import type { ProjectDetail } from '../../data/firma2-project-detail';

interface Props {
  detail: ProjectDetail;
}

const { detail } = Astro.props;
---
<!-- `size="md"` is the app's control rung — the same 40-ish step every field and
     button in this spoke is pinned to, so the tab row reads as chrome of the
     same weight as the controls under it rather than as a smaller sub-navigation.
     `underline` is the default appearance and the right one here: the segmented
     appearance draws a filled track, which would put a second surface inside a
     rail that is already a surface. -->
<esa-tab-layout
  class="firma2-project-rail"
  size="md"
  appearance="underline"
  data-record-tabs
  data-comment-count={detail.comments.length}
>
  <div slot="panel-0">
    <Firma2ProjectFacts detail={detail} />
  </div>
  <div slot="panel-1">
    <Firma2ProjectContacts contacts={detail.contacts} />
  </div>
  <div slot="panel-2">
    <Firma2ProjectComments comments={detail.comments} />
  </div>
</esa-tab-layout>

<style>
  /* BEFORE THE CUSTOM ELEMENT UPGRADES there is no shadow root and therefore no
     panel switching, so all three panels are just three divs in the document —
     the whole rail's content at once, for however long the module takes to
     arrive. `:defined` is the platform's own hook for that window: until the
     element is registered, everything but the first panel is hidden, and the
     moment it is, this rule stops matching and the lego's slot takes over. The
     first panel stays visible throughout, so the rail never renders empty and
     nothing moves when the upgrade lands.

     This is the guard, not a re-implementation of the control: it names no
     tab, holds no state, and does nothing after the element is defined. */
  .firma2-project-rail:not(:defined) > [slot^='panel-']:not([slot='panel-0']) {
    display: none;
  }
</style>

<script>
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-tab-layout';

  // `tabs` is an ARRAY PROPERTY on the lego, not an attribute — it cannot be
  // expressed in markup, which is why the labels live here rather than beside
  // the panels they name. They must stay in step with each panel's own hidden
  // heading (firma2-rail-section's `titleHidden`): the heading is what a screen
  // reader announces for the region, the label is what everyone else reads, and
  // they should be the same word.
  customElements.whenDefined('esa-tab-layout').then(() => {
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('esa-tab-layout[data-record-tabs]').forEach((el) => {
      const comments = Number(el.dataset.commentCount ?? 0);
      (el as HTMLElement & { tabs: unknown }).tabs = [
        { label: 'Key facts' },
        { label: 'Contacts' },
        // Above zero only — see the module header on why an empty thread is the
        // one thing in here worth announcing from outside.
        comments > 0 ? { label: 'Comments', badge: comments } : { label: 'Comments' },
      ];
    });
  });
</script>
